    explain: "### CAGED shapes for any scale\nA CAGED shape is a region of the neck anchored on a chord shape's roots: E and G shapes on string 6, C and A shapes on string 5, D on string 4. Any 7-note scale fills that region with a 2–3 notes-per-string fingering. You already know these for major. For other scales, keep the **roots where they are** and move only the degrees that change: melodic minor lowers each 3rd by a fret, harmonic minor lowers each 3rd and 6th.\n\n| mode | formula | characteristic note | chord |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lydian | 1 2 3 #4 5 6 7 | #4 | maj7#11 |\n| Ionian | 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | 4 (avoid over maj7) / 7 | maj7 |\n| Mixolydian | 1 2 3 4 5 6 b7 | b7 against 3 | 7 |\n| Dorian | 1 2 b3 4 5 6 b7 | 6 | m7, m6 |\n| Aeolian | 1 2 b3 4 5 b6 b7 | b6 | m7(b13) |\n| Phrygian | 1 b2 b3 4 5 b6 b7 | b2 | sus4(b9), m7(b9) |\n| Locrian | 1 b2 b3 4 b5 b6 b7 | b5 | m7b5 |\n\n### Where each mode comes from, and what it's for\n| mode | parent: degree | one note away | played over | where you meet it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ionian | major, 1st | Lydian (4→#4), Mixolydian (7→b7) | maj7, 6 | the I chord: tonic of a major key, end of a ii–V–I |\n| Dorian | major, 2nd | Mixolydian (b3→3), Aeolian (6→b6), melodic minor (b7→7) | m7, m6 | the ii of a ii–V–I; minor-vamp tonics (So What, Oye Como Va) |\n| Phrygian | major, 3rd | Aeolian (b2→2), Locrian (5→b5), Phrygian dominant (b3→3) | m7(b9), sus4(b9) | the iii chord; Spanish-flavoured and metal minor vamps |\n| Lydian | major, 4th | Ionian (#4→4), Lydian dominant (7→b7) | maj7#11 | the IVmaj7 of a major key, borrowed bVImaj7 / bIImaj7; 'floating' film-score tonics |\n| Mixolydian | major, 5th | Ionian (b7→7), Dorian (3→b3), Lydian dominant (4→#4) | 7, 13, 7sus4 | the V7 that resolves to I; blues, funk and rock dominant vamps |\n| Aeolian | major, 6th | Dorian (b6→6), Phrygian (2→b2), harmonic minor (b7→7) | m7(b13) | the vi chord; the natural-minor tonic of pop and rock minor keys |\n| Locrian | major, 7th | Phrygian (b5→5), Locrian nat2 (b2→2), altered (4→b4) | m7b5 | the vii chord of a major key; the iiø7 of a minor ii–V–i |",
