    body: "Martino's *The Nature of the Guitar* treats the **augmented triad** (a triangle: 3 major 3rds of 4 frets) and the **diminished 7th** (a square: 4 minor 3rds of 3 frets) as the guitar's parent shapes. 3 × 4 = 12: he calls the guitar 'multiplication' where the piano is addition (7 + 5). The G–B major 3rd in the tuning lines up with the augmented triad.\n\n### Transformations (one note, one fret)\n- Lower any note of an augmented triad → a major triad (C+ → E, Ab, C).\n- Raise any note → a minor triad (C+ → C#m, Fm, Am).\n- Lower any note of a dim7 → a dominant 7th rooted on that note (Cdim7 → B7, D7, F7, Ab7).\n\nGuy Capuzzo (Music Theory Online 12.1, 2006) shows how closely this matches neo-Riemannian theory.",
